{"input": "", "answers": ["Since we last checked in, the situation in Mali following a soldiers' coup has spiraled, with rising concerns over food and gas shortages, violence, and indeed the country's \"very existence,\" according to Le Monde. An update: The coup has driven more than 200,000 people from their homes to other parts of the region, the AP reports. The UN worries that major food shortages could be imminent, and \"mayhem in these towns and cities is increasing,\" says a rep. Neighboring countries have established an embargo against the rebels who ousted the president; they've closed off their borders and frozen the country's regional bank account. That has prompted residents of Mali, which imports all its fuel, to rush to gas stations to collect fuel. The country's electricity grid may also be headed for failure. UNESCO is citing a threat to Timbuktu, a World Heritage Site full of \"architectural wonders,\" notes the BBC. Mosques there are \"essential to the preservation of the identity of the people of Mali,\" says the group. Yet \"nothing seems to be able to stop\" the Tuareg coup, Le Monde reports. West African leaders need to help Mali's military fight back before the instability spreads beyond the country's borders."], "length": 2503, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "The Tuareg rebels in Mali have taken control of the northern part of the country, including the key towns of Gao, Timbuktu, and Kidal. The rebels are fighting for independence from Mali and the establishment of an independent Tuareg state called Azawad. The Tuareg rebels are divided into two groups: the National Movement for the Liberation of Azawad (MNLA), which wants independence for the Tuareg homelands, and the Ansar Dine, which is linked to the al-Qaeda branch in North Africa and wants to impose Sharia law in the region. The MNLA has taken control of the northern towns, while Ansar Dine has taken control of the capital city of Bamako. The Tuareg rebels have also taken advantage of the power vacuum created by the coup that overthrew President Amadou Toumani Touré. The coup leaders have not sent troops to defend the north from the Tuareg rebels. The international community has condemned the coup and imposed sanctions on Mali. The United Nations has warned that the fighting in Mali could damage the World Heritage Site of Timbuktu. The Tuareg rebels have made rapid advances in the north, taking control of key towns and imposing Islamic law. The rebels have also taken advantage of the power vacuum created by the coup. The international community has offered to help Mali, but the country's neighbors have been slow to respond. The situation in Mali is deteriorating, with the risk of the north becoming an independent state. The international community must offer more support to Mali to prevent the division of the country."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["JK Rowling makes the case today that millions of kids around the world are living in appalling conditions—the stuff of Grimms' fairytales—in institutions that are supposed to be caring for them. Rowling wants to change that, not by improving these orphanages with \"pretty murals\" or teddy bears, but by eliminating them altogether, she writes in the Guardian. The big challenge is providing support for often-poor families who think they have no choice but to place their child in such an institution. If a child's biological family isn't up to the task, then foster families can step in. But Rowling argues that institutions are absolutely the wrong answer. Rowling has started a charity called Lumos (yes, after one of her Harry Potter spells), and writes that it has made genuine progress in reducing new placements in countries such as Bulgaria, Moldova, and the Czech Republic. She wants to raise awareness and donations with campaigns like this one. \"I recently committed to becoming president of Lumos for life,\" writes Rowling. \"It is my dream that, within my lifetime, the very concept of taking a child away from its family and locking it away will seem to belong to a cruel, fictional world.\" Click for her full column."], "length": 1530, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "JK Rowling, founder and president of Lumos, recently launched the awareness campaign #Letstalklumos to end the practice of institutionalizing children. The campaign aims to create awareness and change the future for children in institutions. Lumos estimates that the institutionalization of children can be eradicated globally by 2050. The organization has already seen success in Moldova, the Czech Republic, and Bulgaria, where the number of children in institutions has decreased by 70%, 75%, and 54%, respectively. The EU, US, and Global Alliance for Children have made commitments to end the practice of institutionalizing children. The #Letstalklumos campaign encourages everyone to play a role in ending the practice of institutionalizing children. JK Rowling has committed to becoming president of Lumos for life and hopes that within her lifetime, the concept of taking a child away from its family and locking it away will seem like a cruel, fictional world."}
