{"input": "", "answers": ["Connor Cummins' family describes him to WABC-TV as a photography devotee—and that enthusiasm may have cost him his life. The New Jersey man, said to be either 20 or 24 years old, and an 18-year-old pal ascended the scaffolding outside NYC's Four Seasons hotel Wednesday night in search of the perfect panoramic picture—but Cummins may have slipped on railings made slippery by the rain and fell nine stories to his death, cops tell NJ.com. An NYPD spokesman tells the New York Daily News that the initial investigation suggests the men weren't guests of the hotel. Cummins, who originally hails from Rockaway and whose Facebook page indicates he's now a student at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, per ABC, landed on one of the hotel's lower-level rooftops around 11:15pm and was pronounced dead at the scene, reports the New York Post. His friend wasn't injured and was questioned by authorities to fill in the blanks; he was released Thursday morning without being charged. \"He was a loving boy, that's all I can say,\" one of Cummins' relatives tells the Post. \"I don't know what happened.\" (There's more suspicion surrounding a Muslim teen's fatal fall off a Seattle building.)"], "length": 1299, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "A 20-year-old man from Rockaway, New Jersey, fell to his death from the scaffolding of the 52-story New York City hotel, the Four Seasons Hotel New York City. The man, Connor Cummings, was attempting to take pictures of the city with his friend, Dimitri Oliveras, when he lost his footing and fell nine stories. Cummings was pronounced dead at the scene. Oliveras, who was also from New Jersey, climbed down the ladder and fled the scene in a taxi cab, but later returned to the hotel and called his mother. Cummings was a sophomore psychology major at the University of Massachusetts at Amherst and was also a member of the fraternity Alpha Sigma Phi. He was described as a loving boy who was passionate about photography. Cummings's fraternity brothers posted a message on Facebook mourning his death and described his \"fiery passion\" for photography. A childhood friend of Cummings's said he was a remarkable friend and had an amazing family. He put others first, lived and breathed his passions, and was very down to earth."}
